      <h1>Deals &amp; investment</h1>
      <p class="sl-contract">The figures are sourced. The argument and forecasts are mine.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="sl-stamp">Q3 2026 · Published Jul 24, 2026 · Data through Jun 30, 2026</div>
</section>
  <p class="sl-lede">Mexico's biggest investment number, foreign direct investment, is mostly companies that already operate here leaving last year's profits in the country instead of sending them home. The official registry counts that as reinvested earnings, and it usually carries the quarter. So the headline can set a record in a year when very little new money arrived from outside. Two smaller flows sit beside it, funds and corporate buyers taking over existing Mexican businesses and venture investors funding new ones, plus one physical check on whether any of it became a building: industrial space actually leased.</p>

  <section class="sec">
    <div class="sec-head"><h2>How it works</h2><div class="sec-note">Last revised Aug 1, 2026</div></div>
    <p class="p">Foreign direct investment is the biggest of the three flows and the slowest to move. The Secretaría de Economía, Mexico's economy ministry, publishes it every quarter through the RNIE, the national registry that foreign-owned companies are required to file with. It arrives in three parts that mean very different things. New investment is money from outside buying or building something here. Reinvested earnings are profits a foreign-owned Mexican company made and chose to keep in Mexico. Intercompany accounts are loans between a Mexican subsidiary and its parent abroad, which run in reverse when the subsidiary pays the loan back. The three move for different reasons, so the split tells you more than the total does.</p>
    <p class="p">Two features of the figure make it easy to misread. It is published as a running total for the year, and a quarterly flow is the difference between two of those running totals, so a quarter can come out negative without a single company leaving Mexico: repayments and withdrawals only have to outweigh what came in that quarter. Reinvested earnings also enter only once owners have decided what to do with the previous year's profits, which lands them unevenly through the year rather than in a smooth stream. Both are reasons to compare a quarter with the same quarter a year earlier and never with the quarter before it. The registry then revises the figure for several quarters after it first appears, so the first print is the least reliable one.</p>
    <p class="p">Private equity and mergers sit in the middle, and the buy side is smaller than the deal flow makes it look. The same names recur: a handful of regional funds, family holding companies, and strategic buyers from the United States and Spain. Announced values are usually enterprise value, meaning the whole business including the debt it carries, rather than the cash the buyer actually handed over, and they are quoted in pesos or in dollars depending on who is telling the story. Two reports of the same deal rarely carry the same number, and neither one has to be wrong. For anyone assembling a sense of what Mexican businesses sell for, that means the raw material is in mixed units before the arithmetic even starts.</p>
    <p class="p">Venture capital is the smallest pool and the loudest. Mexico is the second market in the region behind Brazil, concentrated in fintech, logistics and business-to-business commerce. Rounds are announced by the company receiving the money, at the moment that company wants attention, so the news follows fundraising cycles rather than the economy. A quiet quarter for announcements is not evidence that less was invested, only that fewer companies had a reason to say so.</p>
    <p class="p">Industrial real estate is the check on all of it. A plant announcement costs nothing to make, while a signed lease is a company paying rent every month on a building it now has to fill. That shows up in two measurable things: net absorption, the floor space newly occupied minus the space given back, and the vacancy rate in one named corridor such as Monterrey, Saltillo or Guadalajara. Both move roughly six months after the decision was taken, which makes them slow and hard to fake. For a company budgeting its own plant, a corridor where vacancy has fallen is a rent negotiation it is going to lose.</p>
    <p class="p">So announced totals are intentions, and they are the part of this page that will be quoted at you most often. Three things are worth following instead, because they recur and can be checked: whether reinvested earnings hold up in the RNIE split, whether industrial space keeps being absorbed in the corridors where the plants are going, and whether the same funds keep appearing on the buy side.</p>
  </section>

