  <section class="sec">
    <h2>My view</h2>
    <p class="p">Mexico's investment story is told almost entirely in announcements, and announcements are the weakest evidence in it. Two measures are worth more than the headline, I think. Reinvested earnings is a decision taken by people who already run a plant here and know what it costs to run, and industrial absorption is a monthly rent payment rather than a plan. Total foreign direct investment gets quoted in both directions as proof of a boom or of a retreat, but it bundles those reinvested profits together with intercompany loans that can swing a quarter negative on repayments alone, so a large move in the total often says nothing about whether anyone decided to build anything.</p>
    <p class="p">The next few quarters will most likely keep this shape: reinvested earnings carrying the headline, venture rounds staying small and staying in fintech and logistics, and whatever movement matters showing up in leases rather than in launches. The venture side still matters to me more than its size suggests, because it funds the companies that sell payment and logistics infrastructure to Mexican businesses, so a round too small to register nationally can decide who is selling to a merchant next year. For a buyer assembling comparables, the practical consequence sits in the paperwork: announced values in the press mix enterprise value with cash paid and mix pesos with dollars, so a comparison built out of headlines is built out of inconsistent units. The steadier pair is the component split in the RNIE registry, published quarterly by the Secretaría de Economía, alongside corridor vacancy from the industrial brokers.</p>
    <p class="p change-mind"><b>What would change my mind:</b> two consecutive quarters in which the RNIE component split, published quarterly by the Secretaría de Economía, shows the growth carried by new investment rather than by reinvested earnings, with net absorption rising in the same quarters in the industrial brokers' reports. That would mean the announcements are converting into built capacity, and this page should start leading with them.</p>
  </section>

